{"text":"Q3 QUANTIFIED, AND THE TAX IS NOT FLAT - WHICH IS WORSE THAN A LARGE FLAT ONE. 2026-09-23 ~01:20 IST, bench-refresh note 01M35811. Upper bound taken as the turn issuing the failing command plus the next turn, API-equivalent at fitted ratios, totals within 3% of reported costs. claude-sofar 9.8% of session cost ($0.57 of $5.83); claude-automemory 7.0% ($0.33 of $4.76); claude-bare 10.7% ($0.57 of $5.29); Codex 28 of 188 tool outputs carrying the error, 15% (S1 20%, S2 7%); Cursor ZERO in both the smoke and the fork cell. So 2.8 points of pure harness artefact sit between claude-sofar and claude-automemory, directly on C1's central comparison, and a family-sized artefact sits between Cursor and Codex, directly on D19's 'best competitor across families' choice. SOFAR-C3'S RECOMMENDATION FOR THE PACKET, stated as a named position the operator can disagree with: APPLY THE PATCH. The reason is the shape, not the size - a flat 10% tax cancels and can be disclosed, this one does not cancel, it MOVES the answer. Section 1's not-claimable list is for what could not be measured, not for what was measured and left in. Against that the price is about $12 and 35-40 minutes of re-run (claude-sofar S1 plus the claude-drive task; Cursor and Codex need none, their denial evidence under the patched profile already exists from a direct sandbox-exec test), plus an allowance letting an agent list sibling cell NAMES while reading nothing inside. ONE CHECK THAT MAY MAKE THE TRADE FREE: what do the sibling directory names SAY. If the cells root lists claude-sofar, claude-bare, claude-automemory, the leak is not files but the EXPERIMENT - an agent seeing those names can infer it is one arm of an A/B test and which arm - which is a behavioural contamination nastier than reading a sibling's package.json. If so, rename cell directories to opaque ids in the same breath as the patch and Q3 stops being a trade. ALSO ASKED: measure the tax on the REMAINING arms while they run, claude-subagents especially, since a fan-out arm could carry a much larger tax if every subagent trips the same EPERM on its own first directory read - that would be another non-uniformity landing on a different claim again. THIRD DOOR RESOLVED AS A COMPLEMENT, NOT A REPLACEMENT: node, npm and npx/tsc all work under the current profile and only Bun hard-fails, so the drive verify command could move to npm - but CHAIN-A's FROZEN footer mandates bun to the agent, and changing that text changes the pinned workload (CHAIN-A e4e0846529625fc6, D5). Bun is essential to the scenario, not incidental. It fixes the drive gate only, and the packet must say why the hard road was taken or a reader will think it was taken for no reason."}
