{"text":"THE COVERAGE GAP WAS A SCRIPT BUG, AND FIXING IT MADE EVERY TAX FIGURE LARGER. 2026-09-22T20:15Z (01:45 IST on the 23rd), bench-refresh note 01M358V5, which SUPERSEDES the cost tables in 01M35811, 01M3585F, 01M358JQ and 01M358Q6. THE BUG: Claude Code writes several transcript lines per assistant message, one per content block, and `usage` ACCUMULATES across them; the analysis deduplicated by message id keeping the FIRST occurrence. Over the subagents cell first = 19,625 output tokens, last = max = 73,848, adapter modelUsage = 73,848 exactly. So no output was ever missing, the subagents arm is fully measurable, and '26.6% coverage' was the script rather than a property of the arm. The shape diagnosed it: a whole category missing cleanly is a reader-side omission, not lossy capture. CORRECTED TABLE, max per message id, all five arms at 100% coverage, fitted now within 1.7-3.5% of reported where subagents was 29% out - reported cost / artefact / tax: subagents $4.69 / $0.36 / 7.5%; automemory $4.93 / $0.50 / 10.5%; sofar $5.97 / $0.78 / 13.3%; bare $5.45 / $0.78 / 14.7%; drive $7.90 / $1.34 / 17.4%. The tax is therefore LARGER than anything reported earlier tonight - 7.5-17.4%, not 6.7-10.7%. C1's direction is unchanged and still against us (sofar 13.3 against automemory 10.5). C4 is now DETERMINABLE and far worse than stated: drive 17.4 against subagents 7.5, a 9.9-point gap biasing against drive, which is sofar's own flagship arm. Both headline claims biased against us; the chain projection scales past the 10-22% band. This strengthens the Q3 recommendation rather than changing it - apply the patch, fresh paths, and the run is not worth starting without both. THE COVERAGE THRESHOLD IS ADOPTED as a rule, not as care: readable output against adapter modelUsage, below 90% is UNMEASURED, and no direction, bound or ratio may be computed from such a row. It would have caught this row before the first of the three attempts that got it wrong, which is the test of whether a rule earns its place. The Claude-transcript accumulation rule is now repo memory. SOFAR-C3 ASKED THREE THINGS BEFORE THE PACKET. (1) WHERE ELSE HAS THAT SCRIPT RUN - round 1 first: if any round-1 figure was FITTED from transcripts rather than read from the adapter or ledger it is understated the same way, and the chain numbers the artefact has been projected against all night ($30.52-35.57 against $35.58) are exactly that kind of figure; say which source they came from explicitly. Then the other families: the accumulation is a CLAUDE CODE transcript property, so if first-occurrence dedup also ran over Codex and Cursor transcripts that do not accumulate, Claude arms were understated while the others were not - a family-shaped error in every cross-family number, which is where D19 lives. Cursor at 0% is safe because zero is zero; Codex's 15% used a different method and should be re-derived or declared out of scope. (2) STATE THE RESIDUAL: with coverage at 100%, fitted still sits 1.7-3.5% from reported, no longer explained by missing output, so it is a property of the price model or the accounting. An unexplained 3% declared is fine; an unexplained 3% discovered is not. (3) PUT ONE EXPLICIT SUPERSESSION LINE in the packet and the record naming all four superseded tables, because a morning reader will otherwise find the wrong table before the right one."}
