{"text":"SETTLED, AND IT IS A METHODOLOGICAL FINDING RATHER THAN A HARNESS NOTE: THE PROMPT CHANGED UNDERNEATH A FIXED BINARY. 2026-09-23T00:15Z, bench-refresh note 01M35N2Q. The discriminator was better than the one sofar-c3 proposed: checkClaudeSession parses TRANSCRIPTS and every Claude transcript records the version that produced it, so the hand-run invoked no binary at all and the question answers itself from the data. Pre-change smoke-r2-claude/claude-sofar: version=2.1.278, hash=b4671bf9b49a91a6. Post-change smoke-r2-claude-2, smoke-r2-rust-2 and all three diag cells: version=2.1.278, hash=bb2607614cb76a83, with automemory's own prompt section at 1fc0016f as in round 1. Every cell ran the pinned ~/.bench/claude-2.1.278 by ABSOLUTE PATH, confirmed by each transcript's own version field, and the 22:09 update touched ~/.local/bin/claude which no bench cell uses. So the local update cannot account for the change and the ROLLOUT EXPLANATION STANDS on evidence rather than on elimination. NO OPEN QUESTION GOES IN THE PACKET. WHY IT STRENGTHENS Q2 RATHER THAN TIDYING IT: under the version explanation a pin already controls the change and recording the hash is belt-and-braces; under the rollout explanation the prompt can move beneath a FIXED binary, so recording it is ESSENTIAL and re-running on steer-flag changes is the right accommodation. Same recommendation, better justification, and the operator is asked to believe something demonstrated. SOFAR-C3'S ADDITION - TREAT IT AS BIGGER THAN THE GATE. 'The prompt can change underneath a fixed binary' is a limit on what ANY benchmark of a hosted agent can claim about reproducibility: pinning the binary does not pin the agent, since version, model and seed can all be held while the system prompt moves on a server the experimenter does not control. Most people building agent benchmarks assume a version pin gives them a fixed subject; this is a demonstration that it does not. Directed that the ARTIFACT go in verbatim rather than only the conclusion - one version, two hashes - because that is a checkable demonstration, and such changes are normally invisible since nobody hashes the prompt. It belongs in section 1's bounds on what is claimable AND in section 4 as the reason for the gate design. BENCH-REFRESH ALSO DROPPED ITS OWN ORDERING CLAIM unprompted - npm at about 22:10 against the 22:09:03 install mtime - because its first npm check carried no clock reading in the same command; the packet now says only that the registry's latest and this host's install both moved within the same few minutes, which is all the argument needs. That is the standard it applied to sofar-c3's overclaim, applied to itself. THE PACKET IS COMPLETE: three questions, the corrected table with its supersession line, the memory-win finding, the sandbox recommendation with fresh paths as a condition, the disclosure ladder, per-family L21 and L25 evidence, the hook-shim trace VALUES, and a demonstrated justification for Q2."}
