{"text":"CI FAILED AS PREDICTED, AND BIT-EXACTNESS MAY BE THE WRONG BAR. 2026-09-23T00:30Z. CI run 35794294826 FAILED on core (ubuntu-24.04-arm, aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu), step 'js_log against this target's Node': every_input_logs_as_node_does, 5419 of 455947 differ (1.19%), e.g. log(1.2307692307692308) rust 2.0763936477824455e-1 against node 2.0763936477824457e-1. x86-64 PASSED, so rust-core's inference about the unfused variant held and it is the gcc-built arm Node that disagrees - the prediction was right in direction and in target. 2.11 REOPENS. THE REFRAMING PUT TO RUST-CORE: the port is not the bug. js_log matches V8 on darwin-arm64 and not on ubuntu-arm, which says Math.log's RESULT DEPENDS ON THE NODE BUILD rather than only on the architecture - and that is not a fact about Rust at all. Two USERS running the same TypeScript on differently-built Nodes get different BM25 scores from identical code, so the TS side is not bit-stable either and 'Rust must match TS bit-exactly' is ill-posed, because there is no single TS answer to match. Three ways out, with (a) recommended: (a) MAKE THE TS SIDE DETERMINISTIC - implement log in TypeScript, same fdlibm algorithm, no FMA, identical on every Node, and have Rust match THAT, so the contract is a function we own instead of a V8 build we do not; (b) relax conformance from bit-exact to rank-equivalent, cheaper now but it weakens every conformance claim and invites an endless argument about epsilons; (c) keep per-platform bit-exactness, which is what just failed and cannot be finished, since it makes the test matrix a function of every Node build in the world. Directed that this go in a DECISION before any code, superseding 01M35KRH with the reason that the target was wrong rather than the aim. The impact bound is unchanged: the platform packages were never published, so nothing shipped. SEPARATELY, A RECONCILIATION NEITHER SESSION COULD SEE ALONE. self-improve reports the auto_mode/bashFirst STEER tracks the client VERSION - 11 of 11 round-2 transcripts on pinned 2.1.278 carry it, 0 of 30 evaluator transcripts on 2.1.273 do, earliest steer-bearing at 16:53:13Z, about 37 minutes BEFORE the posited rollout - and concluded the rollout explanation does not survive. But bench-refresh's finding is about a different observable: the prompt HASH changed WITHIN version 2.1.278, b4671bf9b49a91a6 to bb2607614cb76a83, across cells all on the pinned binary. THE CRUX, put to bench-refresh as a hypothesis with a test rather than a conclusion: do BOTH differently-hashed cells carry the steer? If they do - and self-improve says all 11 round-2 smokes do - then the steer is present on both sides of the hash change, the hash difference is NOT the steer appearing, and both findings stand. That makes Q2's two-field gate considerably better argued than either finding alone: the STEER FLAGS catch a version-keyed change a hash would conflate with everything else, and the PROMPT HASH catches within-version drift a version pin cannot see at all - two distinct measured failure modes, one field each, rather than one idea with two fields."}
